{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GuFDpTENWAFoBTHksoLCna","type":"tool_result","content":"874\t## Exam Alert! 🚨\n875\t\n876\t**High-Priority Topics**\n877\t\n878\t1. **Filter → score → bind**, in that order. Binding is a notification to the API server.\n879\t2. **The scheduler does not start the container.** The kubelet on the chosen node does.\n880\t3. **Equal scores break at random.**\n881\t4. **An unschedulable Pod stays `Pending`.** Not an error. Nothing times out.\n882\t5. **Filtering fits *requests*** — not limits, not observed usage.\n883\t6. **A Pod is scheduled once and is never rescheduled.** It gets replaced, not moved.\n884\t7. **The three taint effects and their timing** — only `NoExecute` touches running Pods.\n885\t8. **A toleration permits; it does not attract.**\n886\t9. **`nodeName` bypasses the scheduler**, overrules `nodeSelector` and affinity, and fails rather than waiting.\n887\t\n888\t**Common Traps**\n889\t\n890\tYou met the first of these in Chapter 3, where it was defused explicitly and you were told it would come back *[cross-bearing: see Ch 3 §2 — the control plane]*. Here's the piece Chapter 3 held back.\n891\t\n892\t| Trap | The correction |\n893\t|---|---|\n894\t| \"The scheduler places the Pod on the node.\" | It notifies the API server. The kubelet on the chosen node is what starts containers. |\n895\t| \"The scheduler picks the single best node, deterministically.\" | Ties are broken at random [source: k8s-docs-kube-scheduler-2026-08-23]. |\n896\t| \"An unschedulable Pod errors out.\" | It remains unscheduled until the scheduler is able to place it [source: k8s-docs-kube-scheduler-2026-08-23]. |\n897\t| \"A failed Pod is rescheduled to a healthy node.\" | A Pod is never rescheduled to a different node; it is replaced by a new Pod with a different UID [source: k8s-docs-pod-lifecycle-2026-08-23]. |\n898\t| \"A toleration schedules the Pod onto the tainted node.\" | Tolerations allow scheduling but don't guarantee it — the scheduler still evaluates every other parameter [source: k8s-docs-taints-tolerations-2026-08-23]. **The most durable error in this material.** |\n899\t| \"`NoSchedule` evicts the Pods already running.\" | It doesn't. Pods currently running are not evicted [source: k8s-docs-taints-tolerations-2026-08-23]. Only `NoExecute` does. |\n900\t| \"Node affinity moves a Pod when the node's labels change.\" | `IgnoredDuringExecution` — the Pod continues to run [source: k8s-docs-assign-pod-node-2026-08-23]. |\n901\t| \"`PreferNoSchedule` means never.\" | It's a preference; the control plane will try to avoid the node but it is not guaranteed [source: k8s-docs-taints-tolerations-2026-08-23]. |\n902\t| \"`nodeSelector` and affinity are two syntaxes for the same power.\" | Affinity adds soft rules and five operators `nodeSelector` cannot express [source: k8s-docs-assign-pod-node-2026-08-23]. |\n903\t| \"A Pod with `nodeName` set that doesn't fit will wait.\" | It fails, with a reason such as `OutOfmemory` [source: k8s-docs-assign-pod-node-depth-2026-08-24]. The one failure here that isn't `Pending`. |\n904\t\n905\t---\n906\t\n907\t## Practice Questions"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a769b283-7b86-4617-a99f-d77d627d09c9","uuid":"2123382c-5353-4111-b211-e8d5fd1b1ce2","timestamp":"2026-08-24T14:11:05.253Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\ch-07\\draft-v1.md","content":"## Exam Alert! 🚨\n\n**High-Priority Topics**\n\n1. **Filter → score → bind**, in that order. Binding is a notification to the API server.\n2. **The scheduler does not start the container.** The kubelet on the chosen node does.\n3. **Equal scores break at random.**\n4. **An unschedulable Pod stays `Pending`.** Not an error. Nothing times out.\n5. **Filtering fits *requests*** — not limits, not observed usage.\n6. **A Pod is scheduled once and is never rescheduled.** It gets replaced, not moved.\n7. **The three taint effects and their timing** — only `NoExecute` touches running Pods.\n8. **A toleration permits; it does not attract.**\n9. **`nodeName` bypasses the scheduler**, overrules `nodeSelector` and affinity, and fails rather than waiting.\n\n**Common Traps**\n\nYou met the first of these in Chapter 3, where it was defused explicitly and you were told it would come back *[cross-bearing: see Ch 3 §2 — the control plane]*. Here's the piece Chapter 3 held back.\n\n| Trap | The correction |\n|---|---|\n| \"The scheduler places the Pod on the node.\" | It notifies the API server. The kubelet on the chosen node is what starts containers. |\n| \"The scheduler picks the single best node, deterministically.\" | Ties are broken at random [source: k8s-docs-kube-scheduler-2026-08-23]. |\n| \"An unschedulable Pod errors out.\" | It remains unscheduled until the scheduler is able to place it [source: k8s-docs-kube-scheduler-2026-08-23]. |\n| \"A failed Pod is rescheduled to a healthy node.\" | A Pod is never rescheduled to a different node; it is replaced by a new Pod with a different UID [source: k8s-docs-pod-lifecycle-2026-08-23]. |\n| \"A toleration schedules the Pod onto the tainted node.\" | Tolerations allow scheduling but don't guarantee it — the scheduler still evaluates every other parameter [source: k8s-docs-taints-tolerations-2026-08-23]. **The most durable error in this material.** |\n| \"`NoSchedule` evicts the Pods already running.\" | It doesn't. Pods currently running are not evicted [source: k8s-docs-taints-tolerations-2026-08-23]. Only `NoExecute` does. |\n| \"Node affinity moves a Pod when the node's labels change.\" | `IgnoredDuringExecution` — the Pod continues to run [source: k8s-docs-assign-pod-node-2026-08-23]. |\n| \"`PreferNoSchedule` means never.\" | It's a preference; the control plane will try to avoid the node but it is not guaranteed [source: k8s-docs-taints-tolerations-2026-08-23]. |\n| \"`nodeSelector` and affinity are two syntaxes for the same power.\" | Affinity adds soft rules and five operators `nodeSelector` cannot express [source: k8s-docs-assign-pod-node-2026-08-23]. |\n| \"A Pod with `nodeName` set that doesn't fit will wait.\" | It fails, with a reason such as `OutOfmemory` [source: k8s-docs-assign-pod-node-depth-2026-08-24]. The one failure here that isn't `Pending`. |\n\n---\n\n## Practice Questions","numLines":34,"startLine":874,"totalLines":1123}}}
